< {"method":"item/completed","params":{"item":{"type":"reasoning","id":"deepseek_reasoning","summary":[],"content":["Let me analyze the requirements:\n\n1. Build a \"Revenue Command Center\" web application - a real, functional prototype\n2. Focus on customer success, revenue operations, and support leads\n3. Track renewals, billing, tickets, product usage, and incident risks\n4. Round 1: Build project skeleton and first complete product slice\n5. Must use native HTML/CSS/JS or lightweight Node\n6. Must have: top-level business overview metrics, account risk list, at least 3 coherent domains, real interactions (search, filter, detail toggle, status updates), empty/error/high-risk states\n7. Data structured in project, not hardcoded in DOM\n8. Local validation script\n9. Start command binding to 0.0.0.0:5173\n10. Actually run verification\n\nLet me plan the structure:\n\n```\nworkspace/\n├── package.json\n├── server.mjs          # Lightweight Node.js static server (or vite)\n├── scripts/\n│   └── validate.mjs    # Validation script\n├── public/\n│   ├── index.html\n│   ├── css/\n│   │   └── styles.css\n│   ├── js/\n│   │   ├── data.js      # Structured data\n│   │   ├── app.js       # Main app logic\n│   │   ├── components.js # UI components\n│   │   └── utils.js     # Utilities\n│   └── index.html\n└── README.md\n```\n\nActually, let me think about the server. They want `npm start` to bind to `0.0.0.0:5173`. I could use a simple Node HTTP server or use something like `http-server` package. But the constraint says \"light Node\", so let me create a simple Node server script.\n\nLet me think about the data domains:\n1. **Renewals** - upcoming contract renewals, at-risk renewals\n2. **Billing** - overdue invoices, payment failures\n3. **Support** - open tickets, SLA breaches, escalation rate\n4. **Usage** - product usage trends, adoption drops\n5. **Incidents** - service disruptions, degraded performance\n\nAccount risk list with accounts that have risks across these domains.\n\nLet me design the data structure first.\n\nFor the validation script, it should:\n- Check that key files exist\n- Check data fields are present\n- Check DOM text elements\n- Check core business rules\n\nLet me build this step by step.\n\nI'll use a lightweight approach: a simple Node.js static file server that serves the files and binds to 0.0.0.0:5173.\n\nLet me start building:\n\n1. package.json with scripts\n2. server.mjs - simple static file server\n3. public/index.html - main page\n4. public/css/styles.css - styling\n5. public/js/data.js - structured data\n6. public/js/app.js - main app logic\n7. scripts/validate.mjs - validation\n\nLet me think about the data model:\n\nAccounts:\n- id, name, tier, arr, domain (industry), health_score, csm\n- risks: [renewal_risk, billing_risk, support_risk, usage_risk, incident_risk]\n\nRenewals:\n- account_id, contract_end, renewal_status, renewal_amount, risk_flags\n\nBilling:\n- account_id, invoice_id, amount, due_date, status, days_overdue\n\nSupport:\n- account_id, ticket_id, subject, severity, status, created, sla_deadline\n\nUsage:\n- account_id, product, active_users, trend, last_active\n\nIncidents:\n- account_id, incident_id, title, severity, status, started, affected_services\n\nTop metrics:\n- Total ARR\n- At-risk ARR\n- Open tickets\n- Overdue invoices\n- Active incidents\n- Renewal pipeline\n\nLet me build this. I'll make it look professional with a dark dashboard theme.\n\nLet me create all the files now. I'll start with the project structure.\n\nFor the server, I'll create a minimal Node.js HTTP server that serves static files from the `public/` directory and binds to 0.0.0.0:5173.\n\nLet me think about the validation script more carefully. It should:\n1. Verify all key files exist\n2. Load and validate data structure\n3. Verify the data has required fields\n4. Check that index.html includes all required scripts/styles\n5. Verify business rules like \"at-risk accounts must have health_score < 50\" or similar\n\nLet me start coding.\n\nFor styling, I want to create a dark, professional dashboard. Let me think about the color scheme:\n- Dark background: #0a0e17 or similar navy/dark\n- Accent colors for risk levels: red (critical), amber (warning), green (healthy)\n- Cards with subtle borders\n- Modern, clean typography\n\nLet me build this now. I'll create a comprehensive, realistic product.\n\nLet me plan the files I'll create:\n\n1. `package.json` - with scripts\n2. `server.mjs` - Node.js static file server, binds 0.0.0.0:5173\n3. `public/index.html` - main HTML structure\n4. `public/css/styles.css` - all styling\n5. `public/js/data.js` - structured mock data (accounts, renewals, billing, support, usage, incidents)\n6. `public/js/utils.js` - utility functions (formatting, filtering, etc.)\n7. `public/js/components.js` - UI component rendering\n8. `public/js/app.js` - main app initialization, event handling\n9. `scripts/validate.mjs` - validation script\n\nThis is a substantial amount of code but should be achievable. Let me start writing.\n\nFor the server, I'll use a simple approach - a Node.js server that serves static files. I'll avoid using any npm packages to keep it truly \"lightweight Node\". The server will serve files from the `public/` directory.\n\nActually wait - `npm start` needs to work. Let me use `http-server` or just write a bare server. Let me write a bare server since the constraint says \"light Node, no large frameworks\".\n\nLet me get started."]},"threadId":"019e2fa3-65fc-7381-a85d-304bffcc8f7b","turnId":"019e2fa3-6611-75b3-a4b4-77c5da9b8a36","completedAtMs":1778915746572}}
